    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. we've also been running into some really weird, intermittent issues during our QA—like certain UI components just disappearing or buttons being unclickable on certain device models, but we can't replicate it every time. it almost feels like these little bugs are just hiding or waiting to mess things up the second we stop looking. how do you usually track down those kinds of sneaky, unprovoked glitches during an audit?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sneaky, intermittent glitches, especially those that vanish when you look, are the bane of QA, and they often require a more rigorous approach than just standard testing. I'd recommend implementing robust, automated end-to-end testing suites that run constantly in the background, simulating various real-world conditions. Furthermore, you need to drastically improve your logging and crash reporting; when a bug is hiding, you need the system to *tell* you when it's lurking. Think about logging the exact state, device model, OS version, and user action leading up to the disappearance. This data is your best chance at catching the phantom bug.",
      "source": "target",
